f = keep("books", 4,
 kick="Churches need not publish accounts, a political group became a church on paper, and Italy was told to collect unpaid property tax.",
 doubt="The American rule is in the tax code and was reported by ProPublica from government documents. The Italian case is a judgment of the European Union's highest court. The Tirumala figures were published by the temple's own trust, and the Vatican's by its own property office.",
 bridge="The first slide explains the American rule on church accounts and shows a political organisation using it. The second is a regional example from India, where the richest Hindu temple published its wealth for the first time after 89 years, and from the Vatican, which took 54. The third is a regional example from Italy, where the European Union's highest court ordered the government to recover property tax that church-owned businesses had not paid.",
 out="An American church need not publish accounts, and a lobbying group gained the same right by being reclassified as one. A temple and the Vatican went for generations without telling givers what they held. In Italy a court ordered years of unpaid property tax recovered. Exemptions like these matter most to the bodies that own the most. The next chapter looks at the land.")
